
{"id":"30b2ad7d-8054-4d9f-9caa-f036a3498798","object":"chat.completion","created":1785034857,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    min_size = case['min_cluster_size']\\n    clusters = case['clusters']\\n    parent = case['parent']\\n    \\n    cluster_map = {c['cluster_id']: c for c in clusters}\\n    \\n    children = {}\\n    for cid in cluster_map:\\n        pid = parent.get(cid)\\n        if pid is not None:\\n            children.setdefault(pid, []).append(cid)\\n    \\n    ancestors = {}\\n    for cid in cluster_map:\\n        anc = []\\n        p = parent.get(cid)\\n        while p is not None:\\n            anc.append(p)\\n            p = parent.get(p)\\n        ancestors[cid] = anc\\n    \\n    retained = [c for c in clusters if c['size'] >= min_size]\\n    \\n    retained_data = []\\n    for c in retained:\\n        cid = c['cluster_id']\\n        birth = c['birth_lambda']\\n        exits = c['point_exit_lambdas']\\n        stability = sum(max(0, ex - birth) for ex in exits)\\n        retained_data.append({'cluster_id': cid, 'size': c['size'],","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that takes the payload, which contains clusters, parent mapping, min_cluster_size. The task is to compute condensed tree stability, select stable clusters. The output must contain \"clusters\" (array of objects with cluster_id, size, stability) and \"selected_cluster_ids\" (array of strings). The clusters in output are the ones that are retained (size >= min_cluster_size). For each retained cluster, stability = sum(max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda)) over its points? But careful: The input clusters have \"point_exit_lambdas\" array per cluster? Actually the fixture shows each cluster object has \"birth_lambda\" (single number) and \"point_exit_lambdas\" (array of numbers). So each cluster has one birth_lambda and multiple points with exit_lambdas. The stability calculation: For each point in the cluster, compute max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda), sum over points. That gives stability for that cluster.\n\nThen we have parent mapping: parent object keyed by cluster_id, value is parent cluster_id or null for root.\n\nWe need to build a tree. Then drop clusters smaller than min_cluster_size? But note: The condensed tree algorithm typically removes clusters that are smaller than min_cluster_size? In HDBSCAN, after building the condensed tree, clusters are pruned by size. The instruction: \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" So we only keep clusters with size >= min_cluster_size. But we must also consider the hierarchy: The condensed tree is built from the original hierarchy, but we only retain those clusters that meet the size threshold. Then for each retained cluster, compute stability as described. Then rank clusters by decreasing stability, and cluster ID (presumably ascending) as tiebreaker? \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID.\" So sort primarily by stability descending, then by cluster_id ascending.\n\nThen select clusters greedily: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" This is the standard HDBSCAN cluster selection: Iterate over sorted clusters, for each cluster, if none of its descendants (in the full tree? but we only consider retained clusters? The selection should be among the retained clusters. Actually standard algorithm: For each cluster in sorted order, if it has no selected descendant (i.e., no cluster that is a descendant and already selected), then select it, and mark all its ancestors as blocked (cannot be selected later). The \"blocking\" means that when we later consider an ancestor, it will be blocked because it has a selected descendant. So we need to manage a set of selected clusters and a set of blocked clusters.\n\nWe need to determine descendant relationships. The parent mapping gives immediate parent. We can build a tree. Since we have all clusters (including those dropped due to size), we need to consider the full tree for ancestor/descendant relationships. But the selection is only among retained clusters (those with size >= min_cluster_size). However, the blocking condition uses descendants: \"no already selected descendant blocks it\". So if a retained cluster has a descendant that is also retained and already selected, then it's blocked. Also, when we select a cluster, we block its ancestors (any ancestor, not just retained? In HDBSCAN, we block all ancestors, even if they are not retained? But the output only includes retained clusters. So ancestors that are not retained will not be selected anyway, but we still need to mark them as blocked to prevent selection of higher ancestors? Actually the algorithm: For each node (cluster) in the condensed tree (which includes all clusters, but after pruning by size, the tree may have gaps). Typically, the condensed tree includes all clusters that had at least min_cluster_size points at their birth? Wait, the condensed tree is constructed by removing clusters that are below min_cluster_size, and then merging them into parent? Let's recall: In HDBSCAN, the condensed tree is built by considering the hierarchy and pruning clusters that have fewer than min_cluster_size points. But the fixture shows clusters with size 2 which is below min_cluster_size=3. So that cluster should be dropped. However, its parent-child relationships still exist: c2 is child of c0. When we drop c2, we might need to consider that its points are not part of c0's stability? Actually the standard condensed tree algorithm: For each cluster, we compute the number of points that \"fall out\" at each lambda value. The stability is computed from the condensed tree where clusters with fewer than min_cluster_size points are removed and their points are assigned to the parent cluster. But the fixture input seems to already provide clusters with sizes and point exit lambdas. Perhaps the input is the original hierarchy before condensing. The task is to condense it under min cluster size. So we need to apply the condensation: For clusters smaller than min_cluster_size, we remove them and redistribute their points to the parent. The exit lambdas of the points from the child cluster should be added to the parent's point_exit_lambdas? And the child's birth_lambda might be used? Actually standard HDBSCAN condensation: When a cluster is split, the child cluster inherits the parent's points? Let's think carefully.\n\nIn HDBSCAN's condensed tree algorithm, the hierarchy is a tree of clusters where each cluster has a birth level (lambda) and a death level (the lambda when it splits into child clusters). The points in a cluster are those that are members at the birth lambda. As lambda increases, points leave the cluster when they exit (either to become noise or to join a child cluster). In the condensed tree, we only keep clusters that have at least min_cluster_size points at birth. Clusters below that threshold are considered \"spurious\" and their points are merged into the parent cluster (i.e., they are treated as if they never formed a separate cluster). The condensed tree then consists of clusters that have size >= min_cluster_size at birth. For each such cluster, the points that are \"inherited\" or that eventually exit are accounted.\n\nGiven the input structure: each cluster has \"size\" (number of points at birth), \"birth_lambda\", and \"point_exit_lambdas\" - an array of lambda values at which points exited the cluster. But we need to know where those points go: they could become noise or become part of child clusters. The parent mapping indicates the hierarchical relationship: child clusters are \"born\" from parent at some lambda. In the given fixture, c0 is root, c1 and c2 are children of c0. The birth_lambda of c1 is 0.5, which is greater than c0's birth_lambda 0.2. So c1 is a child cluster that splits from c0 at lambda=0.5? Actually the birth_lambda of a child cluster is the lambda at which it appears (i.e., the cluster forms from points that left the parent). That means that the points that become part of child cluster are those that have exit lambda from the parent equal to the child's birth lambda? In HDBSCAN, each point has a series of cluster memberships; the point exits the parent cluster at the lambda where the parent splits into children. So the child's point_exit_lambdas are the lambdas at which those points exit the child cluster (either to go to further descendants or to become noise). The parent's point_exit_lambdas include the birth lambdas of its children? Typically, in the condensed tree, each cluster has an array of \"lambdas\" where points leave: one lambda per point, and the lambda at which it leaves to a child cluster is the child's birth lambda. So the parent's point_exit_lambdas will include the birth lambdas of its children (plus possibly noise points). But in the given fixture, the parent's point_exit_lambdas are [0.4, 0.6, 0.9] for c0, and c1's birth_lambda is 0.5, which is not in that list. So maybe the provided data is not exactly the condensed tree but the original hierarchy? The root c0 has point_exit_lambdas [0.4,0.6,0.9] – these are lambdas at which points leave c0. But c1 appears at 0.5, which is not one of those. So either the data is simplified or the interpretation is different.\n\nGiven the task description: \"Condense a supplied hierarchy under a minimum cluster size and select stable clusters with deterministic tie handling.\" So we need to implement the condensation process. The input gives clusters with size, birth_lambda, and point_exit_lambdas. And a parent mapping. This likely represents the full hierarchy (the \"tree\" before condensation). The condensation step: remove clusters with size < min_cluster_size. For each removed cluster, its points are reassigned to its parent. Specifically, the parent should inherit the points that were in the child. That means the parent's size will effectively increase by the child's size? But the condensation algorithm in HDBSCAN does not simply increase the parent's size; it recomputes the stability for the parent based on the points that end up in it after removing spurious clusters. The stability for a cluster is computed from its \"lambda death\" and the exit lambdas of its points. When a child cluster is removed, its points are treated as if they never left the parent, so their exit lambdas become part of the parent's set of exit lambdas. Also, the parent's birth lambda remains the same.\n\nSo we need to process the tree bottom-up, removing clusters that are smaller than min_cluster_size. For each such cluster, we take its points (size number of points with their exit lambdas) and add them to the parent's point_exit_lambdas (extend the list). Also, we should remove the child from the parent mapping. After removing all small clusters, we have a condensed tree with only clusters that have size >= min_cluster_size after possible addition of points from children. But note: When we add points from a removed child to the parent, the parent's size increases. That could cause the parent to now meet the min_cluster_size if it didn't before? But the condensation process is usually done recursively: start at leaves, if a cluster is too small, absorb it into parent, then after absorption, the parent's size may increase. Then we need to re-evaluate the parent? However, in HDBSCAN, the condensed tree is built by traversing the original hierarchy and at each node, if the cluster's size (including points inherited from below) is less than min_cluster_size, then the whole cluster is considered \"noise\" and all its points are assigned upward. This is done recursively. The typical algorithm processes the tree from leaves to root, maintaining for each node a list of \"points\" with their exit lambdas (or just the number of points and their death lambdas). The decision to keep a node is based on its count after incorporating points from children that were absorbed. So we need to simulate that.\n\nGiven the complexity, but the fixture is relatively simple: three clusters, root has size 7, child c1 size 4, child c2 size 2. min_cluster_size=3. So c2 is too small (size 2). We should absorb c2 into its parent c0. That means c0's size becomes 7+2=9, and c0's point_exit_lambdas becomes original [0.4,0.6,0.9] plus the point_exit_lambdas of c2: [1.1]. Also c1 has size 4 >=3, so it remains. But note: c1 is also a child of c0. Its points are separate. After absorption, we have c0 (size 9) and c1 (size 4). But we also need to consider that c1's points originally left c0 at lambda=0.5? The birth_lambda of c1 is 0.5. In the condensed tree, when we keep c1, the parent c0's point_exit_lambdas should include the lambda at which points left to form c1 (i.e., c1's birth_lambda). But in the original data, c0's point_exit_lambdas are [0.4,0.6,0.9], which does not include 0.5. So maybe the input already accounts for that? Alternatively, the condensation might be simpler: we just drop clusters below min_cluster_size, compute stability from the given point_exit_lambdas, and then select clusters. But the example fixture has three clusters, all with sizes. The solution likely expects that we drop c2 and then compute stability for c0 (including c2's points? Or not?).\n\nLet's examine the output schema: it expects an array of clusters with cluster_id, size, stability, and selected_cluster_ids. The size in the output should be the size after condensation? Or the original size? The task says \"Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size.\" That might mean we simply filter out clusters with size < min_cluster_size, but we don't modify other clusters. The sizes in the output would be the original sizes. Then stability is computed for each retained cluster using its original point_exit_lambdas. Then selection based on that. That would be simpler. The fixture input has clusters with sizes and point_exit_lambdas. If we just drop c2, we have c0 and c1. Compute stability for c0: sum(max(0, 0.4-0.2)=0.2, max(0,0.6-0.2)=0.4, max(0,0.9-0.2)=0.7) = 1.3. For c1: birth=0.5, exits=[0.7,1.0,1.2] => stabilities: 0.2,0.5,0.7 => total 1.4. So c1 has higher stability. Then rank: c1 (stability 1.4), c0 (1.3). Selection: start with c1, it has no selected descendant (since c1 is leaf and c2 is dropped), so select c1, block ancestors: block c0. Then c0 is considered next but it's blocked, so not selected. Result: selected_cluster_ids = [\"c1\"]. clusters output includes both c0 and c1 (since both were retained). That seems plausible.\n\nBut we must also consider that the tree has parent-child relationships. In selection, we need to check descendant relationships. c0 is parent of c1, so c0 is ancestor, c1 is descendant. So when c1 is selected, c0 is blocked. That matches.\n\nNow, what about the point_exit_lambdas? Is it correct to treat them as per-point exit lambdas? The field is \"point_exit_lambdas\" - an array of length equal to size? In the fixture, c0 size=7 but only 3 exits? That suggests that not all points exit the cluster? In HDBSCAN, points that never leave the cluster (i.e., are still present at the maximum lambda) are considered infinite death. In stability, the death for such points is infinity? Actually stability formula: for each point, lambda_max - lambda_birth, but in condensed tree, points that never exit are assigned a death lambda of infinity. So max(0, exit - birth) would be positive for any exit > birth. If a point never exits, we set exit to a large number? Maybe the input provides only the exit lambdas for points that do exit; the remaining points persist until the end. In that case, stability for those points would be (max_lambda - birth_lambda). But we don't have max_lambda. Alternatively, the input might already include all points, with some lamdas being very high? In the given data, c0 has 3 exit lambdas for size 7